<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques Necker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1732">1732</a>–<a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques Necker was a Genevan banker and statesman who served as finance minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1732, he grew rich as a banker in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and in 1777 was made director-general of the royal treasury, as his Protestant faith barred him from the post of Controller-General. In 1781 he published the Compte rendu, making the royal budget public, and was dismissed within months. Recalled in 1788 amid fiscal crisis, he doubled the representation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a> at the Estates-General; his dismissal on 11 July 1789 helped bring about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storming-of-the-bastille">Storming of the Bastille</a>. Recalled within two days, he resigned in September 1790 and died in 1804.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1779 the reforms of <b>Jacques Necker</b>, the director-general of finances, abolished serfdom on the crown’s own lands, but not on those of private lords or the church.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li></ul></section>
